3.5 Documents = student level
A more useful comparison is by who is speaking. Do graduate students talk about different things than undergraduates?

tfidf_level &lt;- tokens |&gt;
  filter(!is.na(level)) |&gt;
  count(level, word) |&gt;
  bind_tf_idf(word, level, n)

tfidf_level |&gt;
  group_by(level) |&gt;
  filter(n &gt;= 5) |&gt;
  slice_max(tf_idf, n = 10, with_ties = FALSE) |&gt;
  ungroup() |&gt;
  ggplot(aes(tf_idf, reorder_within(word, tf_idf, level))) +
  geom_col() +
  scale_y_reordered() +
  facet_wrap(~level, scales = "free") +
  labs(x = "tf-idf", y = NULL)






Figure 3.2: Words most distinctive of each student level.




The new pieces here:


filter(!is.na(level)) drops answers where the student’s level is missing (NA). The ! means “not,” so this reads “keep rows where level is not missing.”

bind_tf_idf(word, level, n) now treats each level (undergraduate, graduate, professional) as a document.

filter(n &gt;= 5) keeps only words used at least five times within that group. Without it, TF-IDF’s top words are dominated by words used once, by one student, in one level. That is a known weakness: rare words get high idf, even when rarity just means “a typo” or “a name.” A minimum count is the standard guard, and you can raise or lower the 5.

Why the professional panel is empty. The professional panel lists words but shows no bars. With only three documents (the three levels), any word that appears in all three has an idf of \ln(3/3) = 0. The professional group is so small that the only words its students use five or more times are everyday words that every group uses, so every one of them scores zero. This is the “common is not distinctive” point from the previous section, combined with the small-group problem in the warning below. Lowering the minimum to n &gt;= 2 would make bars appear, but they would reflect one or two students, not the group.






NoteIn this synthetic data, the differences are noise



Student level was assigned to answers at random when the synthetic data were generated, so graduate and undergraduate students do not truly talk about different things here. The chart still shows a “top ten” for each group, because TF-IDF always returns something. That is worth remembering with real data: a ranked list of distinctive words is not evidence that the groups differ. Read the words in context (below) and ask whether a story survives.








WarningGroup sizes are very different



There are roughly 1,000 undergraduates and fewer than 20 professional students. TF-IDF is a ratio, so it does not know that the professional group is tiny. Words that rank high for a group of 17 people may reflect two or three students. Treat small-group results as leads to follow up by reading, not as findings.

4.2 Choosing the documents
LDA learns from words that appear together within a document. Our answers have a median of about a dozen words, and after stop-word removal, six or seven. That is too little for patterns of togetherness to show up.
The fix is to make the document bigger. We combine each student’s answers (strength, concern, and ideas) into one document. Each student’s document then has a few dozen words, enough to reveal which words travel together.

stops &lt;- stop_words |&gt; filter(lexicon == "snowball")
custom_stops &lt;- tibble(word = c("vcu", "student", "students", "like",
                                "get", "just", "really", "also", "can",
                                "e", "g", "lot"))

tokens &lt;- vcu |&gt;
  unnest_tokens(word, text) |&gt;
  anti_join(stops, by = "word") |&gt;
  anti_join(custom_stops, by = "word") |&gt;
  filter(!str_detect(word, "^\\d+$"))

# Keep words used in at least 5 different students' answers
vocab &lt;- tokens |&gt;
  distinct(resp_id, word) |&gt;
  count(word) |&gt;
  filter(n &gt;= 5) |&gt;
  pull(word)

student_counts &lt;- tokens |&gt;
  filter(word %in% vocab) |&gt;
  count(resp_id, word)

# Drop students left with too few words to say anything
keep &lt;- student_counts |&gt;
  group_by(resp_id) |&gt;
  filter(sum(n) &gt;= 5) |&gt;
  ungroup()

n_distinct(keep$resp_id); n_distinct(keep$word)

[1] 1236


[1] 419


This block does four jobs.


Tokenize and clean (the first tokens &lt;- step). It is the same as in the last two chapters: split into words, remove stop words and bare numbers. (We added “e,” “g,” and “lot” to the domain stop words because they were noise.)

Build a vocabulary (vocab). distinct(resp_id, word) keeps one row per student-word pair, so a word used five times by one student still counts once. count(word) then counts how many different students used each word, and filter(n &gt;= 5) keeps words used by at least five students. pull(word) turns the result into a plain list of words. This pruning is standard: a word used by only one or two students cannot help define a topic, and it slows the model down.

Count words per student (student_counts). filter(word %in% vocab) keeps only the vocabulary words, and count(resp_id, word) counts each word within each student. This is our “documents” table: one row per student-word pair, with a count.

Drop thin documents (keep). group_by(resp_id) followed by filter(sum(n) &gt;= 5) keeps only students with at least five total words left. Inside a grouped filter(), sum(n) is calculated within each student, not across the whole table.

The last line reports how many students (documents) and how many distinct words (the vocabulary) remain. n_distinct() counts unique values.

4.8 Always read the documents
The word lists are a summary of a summary. For each topic, read the documents where it is strongest.

student_text &lt;- vcu |&gt;
  group_by(resp_id) |&gt;
  summarise(text = paste(text, collapse = " | "), .groups = "drop")

gamma |&gt;
  filter(topic == 1) |&gt;
  slice_max(gamma, n = 3, with_ties = FALSE) |&gt;
  left_join(student_text, by = "resp_id") |&gt;
  select(gamma, text)

# A tibble: 3 × 2
  gamma text                                                                    
  &lt;dbl&gt; &lt;chr&gt;                                                                   
1 0.989 Good small class sizes, the TRIO program kept me on track My advisor me…
2 0.983 Really happy with the system, compared to other schools, the how things…
3 0.981 Really happy with the people. Compared to other schools, the email syst…



The first part goes back to the original answers and glues each student’s answers into one string, separated by " | " (paste(..., collapse = " | ")). .groups = "drop" tells summarise() to ungroup afterward.
The second part picks topic 1, keeps its three strongest students, and uses left_join() to attach each student’s text. A left join adds columns from a second table by matching on a shared column (resp_id), keeping every row of the first table.

Change topic == 1 to each topic in turn. If the top documents for a topic do not look related, the topic is not a theme, no matter how coherent its word list looks.

4.9 A check against the interviewers’ codes
We have an independent signal: the topics the interviewers ticked. If LDA is finding real themes, a topic’s strongest documents should tend to carry related interviewer codes.

codes &lt;- vcu |&gt;
  filter(!is.na(topics)) |&gt;
  separate_rows(topics, sep = ";\\s*") |&gt;
  distinct(resp_id, topics)

base_rate &lt;- codes |&gt;
  count(topics) |&gt;
  mutate(base = n / n_distinct(codes$resp_id)) |&gt;
  select(topics, base)

# Only students from the original form have interviewer codes, so compare
# like with like: the size of each topic is counted among coded students only
topic_sizes &lt;- dominant |&gt;
  filter(resp_id %in% codes$resp_id) |&gt;
  count(topic, name = "n_students")

dominant |&gt;
  inner_join(codes, by = "resp_id") |&gt;
  count(topic, topics) |&gt;
  left_join(topic_sizes, by = "topic") |&gt;
  left_join(base_rate, by = "topics") |&gt;
  mutate(share = n / n_students, lift = share / base) |&gt;
  filter(n &gt;= 5) |&gt;
  group_by(topic) |&gt;
  slice_max(lift, n = 3, with_ties = FALSE) |&gt;
  ungroup() |&gt;
  ggplot(aes(lift, reorder_within(topics, lift, topic))) +
  geom_col() +
  geom_vline(xintercept = 1, linetype = "dashed") +
  scale_y_reordered() +
  scale_x_continuous(breaks = c(0, 1, 2)) +
  facet_wrap(~ topic, scales = "free_y", ncol = 2) +
  labs(x = "Lift over the overall rate (1 = no association)", y = NULL)






Figure 4.4: For each LDA topic, the interviewer codes most over-represented among its students.




This is the longest block so far, but it follows one idea: for each LDA topic, which interviewer codes show up more than usual?


codes: the interviewers’ codes are stored as one text string per answer (“Advising; Safety”). separate_rows(topics, sep = ";\\s*") splits that string so each code gets its own row, and distinct(resp_id, topics) keeps one row per student-code pair.

base_rate: how common each code is overall, as a share of coded students.

topic_sizes: how many coded students have each LDA topic as their strongest one.

The main pipeline: inner_join() matches each student’s dominant topic with their codes (an inner join keeps only students who appear in both tables). Then count(topic, topics) counts students for each topic-code pair; left_join() attaches the topic sizes and base rates; and mutate() computes


share: the fraction of that LDA topic’s students who have the code, and

lift: share / base, the ratio of that fraction to the overall rate.



filter(n &gt;= 5) ignores combinations seen fewer than five times, and slice_max(lift, n = 3) keeps the three codes with the highest lift per topic.
The chart draws the lifts as bars. geom_vline(xintercept = 1, linetype = "dashed") adds a dashed line at 1, where a code is exactly as common as usual.

Lift is the key idea. A lift of 2 means the code is twice as common among this topic’s students as in the data overall. A lift of 1 means no relationship. A topic with no codes above 1, or with odd ones, is a candidate for being an artifact.

4.9.1 Reading the chart from our run
Each panel is an LDA topic, and the three bars are the interviewer codes most over-represented among that topic’s students. The dashed line at 1 means “no association,” and a bar that reaches 2 means the code is twice as common as usual.
What the chart tells us is sobering, and that is the point of the check:


Every bar is above 1, but only modestly. Most lifts sit between about 1.2 and 1.8. The largest, in topic 4 (financial aid and health support), reach about 2. These are weak associations. If the topics were capturing the same themes the interviewers coded, we would expect some bars to reach well above 2.

The largest lifts belong to rare codes. “Staff development” and “faculty development” (topics 1 and 2) are among the least-used codes, so only a handful of students carry them. With so few students, a ratio can swing a lot, and the minimum of five students per combination does not fully protect against that.

The pairings do not tell a coherent story. Topic 4, for instance, is the note-taking-style topic, yet its strongest links are to financial aid and health support, which have nothing to do with its words. That is the signature of a topic that reflects phrasing more than subject.

So the interviewers’ codes give us no reason to trust these six topics as themes. This is the validation step doing its job: it protects you from reporting a tidy-looking topic list that does not correspond to anything else you know about the data. (Remember that the text here is synthetic and built from templates. With real interview data, you would hope to see stronger links, and if you did not, this chart is where you would find out.)

6.5 Which words drive the score?
Before trusting a sentiment score, find out which words produce it.

tokens |&gt;
  inner_join(bing, by = "word") |&gt;
  count(word, sentiment, sort = TRUE) |&gt;
  group_by(sentiment) |&gt;
  slice_max(n, n = 12, with_ties = FALSE) |&gt;
  ungroup() |&gt;
  ggplot(aes(n, reorder_within(word, n, sentiment))) +
  geom_col() +
  scale_y_reordered() +
  facet_wrap(~sentiment, scales = "free_y") +
  labs(x = "Count", y = NULL)






Figure 6.3: Words that contribute most to positive and negative scores.




This joins every word to the lexicon, counts how often each sentiment word appears, keeps the twelve most frequent positive and twelve most frequent negative words, and draws them in two panels. It is the “ingredients list” of our scores.
Read this chart skeptically. Look for words that the lexicon scores one way but that mean something else here. Two words stand out. “Support” is the most common positive word, but in “student support services” it is the name of a program, not an opinion. And “like” also ranks among the top positive words, yet it appears in “doesn’t like,” where it means the opposite, and in comparisons (“events like the welcome fair”), where it is a preposition. Whenever a topic word or a function word carries a sentiment label, you are measuring the topic, or the grammar, not the feeling.

7.5 Step 3: Turn text into numbers
A statistical model cannot read words. It needs numbers: a matrix with one row per answer and one column per word. We use TF-IDF weights from Chapter 3. The key discipline is that the vocabulary and the idf values come only from the training data, and the test set is converted using those same values.

stops &lt;- stop_words |&gt; filter(lexicon == "snowball")

tokenize &lt;- function(d) {
  d |&gt;
    select(text_id, text) |&gt;
    unnest_tokens(word, text) |&gt;
    anti_join(stops, by = "word") |&gt;
    filter(!str_detect(word, "^\\d+$"))
}

train_tok &lt;- tokenize(train)

# Vocabulary: words that appear in at least 5 training answers
vocab_df &lt;- train_tok |&gt;
  distinct(text_id, word) |&gt;
  count(word, name = "df") |&gt;
  filter(df &gt;= 5) |&gt;
  mutate(idf = log(nrow(train) / df))

length(vocab_df$word)

[1] 376

# Convert any set of answers to a TF-IDF matrix over the training vocabulary
featurize &lt;- function(d) {
  tok &lt;- tokenize(d) |&gt;
    count(text_id, word) |&gt;
    inner_join(vocab_df, by = "word") |&gt;
    group_by(text_id) |&gt;
    mutate(tf = n / sum(n)) |&gt;
    ungroup()

  sparseMatrix(
    i = match(tok$text_id, d$text_id),
    j = match(tok$word, vocab_df$word),
    x = tok$tf * tok$idf,
    dims = c(nrow(d), nrow(vocab_df)),
    dimnames = list(NULL, vocab_df$word)
  )
}

x_train &lt;- featurize(train)
x_test  &lt;- featurize(test)
dim(x_train); dim(x_test)

[1] 1529  376


[1] 368 376


This block has the most new code so far. It is easier to read as three pieces.
Piece 1: a reusable function, tokenize(). We have been writing steps one at a time; here we package a series of steps as our own function so we can reuse it. function(d) { ... } defines a recipe that takes a table d, and the lines inside the braces { } are the steps. This one keeps the ID and text columns, splits into words, removes stop words, and removes numbers. Whatever the last line produces is what the function returns. Once defined, tokenize(train) runs the whole recipe on the training data.
Piece 2: the vocabulary, from training data only. train_tok holds the training words. Then:


distinct(text_id, word) keeps one row per answer-word pair, and count(word, name = "df") counts how many different answers contain each word. We name that count df (for document frequency).

filter(df &gt;= 5) keeps only words found in at least five training answers.

mutate(idf = log(nrow(train) / df)) computes each word’s IDF, the rarity score from Chapter 3, using the number of training answers. log() is the natural logarithm.

We then print how many words survive.
Piece 3: a second function, featurize(), that builds the matrix. It takes any set of answers d and produces a TF-IDF matrix using the training vocabulary.

The first half counts words per answer, keeps only vocabulary words (inner_join(vocab_df, by = "word")), and computes each word’s TF (tf = n / sum(n), the share of the answer’s words that this word is, calculated within each answer thanks to group_by(text_id)). It then has everything needed for TF-IDF.

sparseMatrix() builds the matrix. You tell it, for each non-zero cell, which row (i), which column (j), and what value (x). Here i is the answer’s position, j is the word’s position in the vocabulary (match() finds a position by lookup), and x is tf * idf, the TF-IDF score. dims fixes the matrix’s size and dimnames labels the columns with the words.

We then build one matrix for training and one for testing, and print their sizes (dim() gives rows and columns). Notice they have the same number of columns but different numbers of rows.
Both matrices have the same columns, which is what lets a model trained on one score the other. Words in the test set that never appeared in training are simply ignored: the model has nothing to say about them.
The matrix is sparse: nearly every cell is zero, since each answer uses only a handful of the few hundred vocabulary words. Storing it as a sparse matrix keeps it small.
